WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.660
The rank of a matrix. Today we will look at
the rank of a matrix and also

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.380
go through ways of finding it.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:16.300
The rank of a matrix equals the largest order
of a nonzero minor of the matrix.

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:22.840
It is denoted rang A. For a given square
matrix A, we need to find its rank.

5
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.197
We find the values of all determinants of this
matrix,

6
00:00:26.197 --> 00:00:29.740
starting with the third-order determinant,
which is zero,

7
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:36.160
and then move on to the second-order
determinants. By the definition of the rank,

8
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.080
in our case the rank of the original matrix
equals two.

9
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.640
I would like to point out that every matrix
has a rank.

10
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.771
To compute the rank of a matrix,

11
00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:55.180
Gaussian elimination is used. It relies on the
fact that the rank

12
00:00:55.180 --> 00:01:01.800
does not change under the following
transformations. First, swapping rows. Second,

13
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:08.420
multiplying the entries of a row by a nonzero
number. Third,

14
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.820
deleting a row whose entries are all zero.
Fourth,

15
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:18.660
dividing the entries by a nonzero number.
Fifth, adding to the entries of any row

16
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.120
the corresponding entries of another row
multiplied by the same number.

17
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:31.130
The idea of Gaussian elimination is to bring
the matrix to row echelon form. Then

18
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:36.330
the rank of the matrix equals the number of
nonzero rows of the echelon matrix.

19
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.962
Let us look at an example: we need to find the

20
00:01:40.962 --> 00:01:44.180
rank of matrix A. To check the calculations

21
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.700
during elementary transformations, we add to
the matrix

22
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:56.560
a column of check sums, each equal to the sum
of the entries in its row.

23
00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.324
We perform elementary transformations on the
new

24
00:02:00.324 --> 00:02:03.460
matrix. If the calculations are correct,

25
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:08.051
then after each transformation the sum of the
entries in every

26
00:02:08.051 --> 00:02:12.420
row must equal the corresponding entry of the
extra column.

27
00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:18.860
Swapping the first and third rows of the
matrix, we get the following matrix.

28
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.339
Next, add to the entries of the second row the
corresponding

29
00:02:22.339 --> 00:02:25.180
entries of the first row multiplied by minus
one,

30
00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:30.731
and to the entries of the third row the
entries

31
00:02:30.731 --> 00:02:35.810
of the first row multiplied by minus three.

32
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.790
Checking each step, we get this.

33
00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:45.280
Add the second row of the last matrix to the
corresponding entries of the third row.

34
00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:51.600
We get the following matrix. Thus the original
matrix A

35
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.952
is equivalent to the matrix reduced to echelon
form,

36
00:02:54.952 --> 00:02:58.240
and the rank of the last matrix is three.
Therefore

37
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.380
the rank of the original matrix equals the
same number.

38
00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.768
To sum up: in this lesson we learned the
definition of

39
00:03:08.768 --> 00:03:13.370
the rank of a matrix and how it can be
computed.
